92186045417] [17592186116308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116308 #edb/ref 1061 456962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f57fbc4c-eee5-581c-8834-5f24fb232ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116309 #edb/ref 1061 456963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "092606ce-b909-5d31-922b-2e79fb7b59c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116310 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116310 #edb/ref 1061 456966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "691855a0-b125-5407-ac61-46a978d8c689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116311 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116311 #edb/ref 1061 456969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116311 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afebb130-5563-5b16-9d8e-fa9f724ad05c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116311 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116312 #edb/ref 1061 456973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7059010-035b-50fa-b4f2-ccd4b5b1eb28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116453 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f7de9810f4acf33b504eb010d641e99811de82eed919e4340b5e68d414269ca.png)\n\nThe area of this right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 ab.\n$$\n\nBut how can we calculate the area if we only know one side and one angle, as in the triangle below? \n\n![](images/c1/c1e5331333639f4d8e4f7b4cb3fee30ed1a7a889968844ae2b10961c5ae8e3ef.png)\n\nWe can follow the two steps below.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the missing side using the trigonometric ratios.\n\nHere, we need the adjacent side, and we know the opposite side. So, we should use tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ BC\\sqrt{3} & =9 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ BC & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Use the formula to calculate the area.\n\nNow that we know both sides of the triangle, we can just apply the formula as usual and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116453 #edb/ref 1065 2458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116453 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "96ace952-d580-563b-85f2-45a48227303d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116453 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428287 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428287 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1608->1397. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045